content.thinking:
"[404]: GET /wp-login.php - Bad file descriptor" — the php -S built-in server returns `false` from the router for wp-login.php (it's a real file, PHP extension → `return false` with PHP_SELF set) — and php -S then tries to serve the file itself... "Bad file descriptor" suggests the static-file serving path failed. Hmm — but wp-admin/admin-post.php is mentioned in the router comment as working... The mail-log/note flows in the harness worked via /wp-admin/admin-ajax.php?? Wait — the earlier harness runs: the checkout race uses /?wc-ajax=checkout (routed through index.php) and the REST API /wp-json/... (through index.php). Did ANY test hit /wp-admin/*.php directly? verify-woo-state.php is wp-cli, not HTTP. The race script: no admin. So this is the FIRST exercise of /wp-admin/*.php over the php -S server — and "Bad file descriptor" breaks it.

Why would php -S fail to serve an existing PHP file? "Bad file descriptor" on php -S with `return false`... Known php -S quirk: with a router script returning false, php -S serves the requested file as if it were static — for .php files it should execute them. Actually when the router returns false, the built-in server serves the requested resource with its default handler — PHP files ARE executed (that's standard). "Bad file descriptor" is odd...

Hmm wait — maybe the issue is the doc root. php -S was started with `cwd: WORDPRESS_DIR` and router.php path — docroot = wordpress/ (not .build/wp). With return false, php -S looks up the file relative to DOCROOT: wordpress/wp-login.php — doesn't exist → 404 "Bad file descriptor"?? The router sets `$_SERVER['PHP_SELF']` and returns false expecting the server to serve `$wp_root.$uri` — but the server resolves against docroot (wordpress/), where wp-login.php does NOT exist. For STATIC files, Woo assets: /wp-content/... — also under docroot?? wordpress/wp-content EXISTS in the repo (that's the source!) — so static assets resolve against the repo's wp-content — that's why the design works (docroot=wordpress/, repo wp-content synced into .build/wp/wp-content by bootstrap). And core admin PHP files: wordpress/wp-login.php doesn't exist → 404.

Fix options:
(a) In the router, for real PHP endpoints: `require $wp_root . $uri;` and exit — i.e., execute the file directly from the router instead of returning false. But then relative requires inside wp-login.php resolve... wp-login.php does `require __DIR__ . '/wp-load.php'` → __DIR__ = .build/wp → fine! chdir issues: PHP's cwd stays wordpress/ — wp-login uses __DIR__ requires; wp-admin/admin-ajax.php: `define('WP_ADMIN', true); require_once __DIR__ . '/wp-load.php'`... but wp-load requires wp-config which uses __DIR__... Should work — many setups do exactly this. The existing router already sets PHP_SELF for these. Change:
```php
if ( '/index.php' !== $uri ) {
    // Real PHP endpoints (wp-admin/*.php, wp-login.php) execute directly,
    // exactly as under a configured web server.
    $_SERVER['PHP_SELF'] = $uri;
    chdir( dirname( $wp_root . $uri ) );
    require $wp_root . $uri;
    exit;
}
```
chdir to the file's dir (wp-admin or root) so relative operations (fopen of relative paths) behave like a normal docroot install.

Hmm wait — but changing the router changes shared harness behavior used by previous issues' tests (Home contract etc. — they don't hit wp-admin PHP files; safe).

But careful — one subtlety: php -S + require from router: $_SERVER['SCRIPT_NAME'] etc. — WP uses PHP_SELF (set) and SCRIPT_NAME for some redirects. php -S sets SCRIPT_NAME='/router.php'... hmm, when the router requires a file, SCRIPT_NAME stays '/router.php'?? That could break wp-admin canonical redirects (redirect_to based on REQUEST_URI usually). WP's wp_guess_url uses REQUEST_URI / PHP_SELF / SCRIPT_NAME. Since the siteurl is exactly http://127.0.0.1:8091 and requests carry the right REQUEST_URI, it should be fine. The existing comment says this router approach was already designed for admin-post.php — meaning "return false" was INTENDED to work... maybe it worked when docroot was different? The harness starts: `spawn(PHP, ['-S', `127.0.0.1:${PORT}`, join(HERE,'router.php')], { cwd: WORDPRESS_DIR })` — docroot=cwd=wordpress. Yeah — return false can't work for .build/wp files. The comment was aspirational (never exercised until now — exactly the kind of gap this issue's testing reveals).

Let me implement the require+exit fix and test wp-login directly.

